KG42.register("rules-92ed92756c51129375683a52",{"id":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives::v141-c-R003","paper":"16-cooperative_microgrid_incentives","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Price-signal coordination","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"Repeat regulated-price updates and consumers’ responses, with the models solved again for power dispatch, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. Repeat the separate utility–microgrid decision iteration, in which the microgrid updates its decisions in response to the resulting regulated utility price, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. Regulators may introduce feed-in premiums on top of market prices to promote microgrid development and efficient operation, including higher production at peak hours. Scope: Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.price_signal under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.price_signal"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models. | Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}. | Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14","v141-b-g05-e06","v141-b-g05-e07"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","symbol":"P","meaning":"Regulated electricity price in the price-signal coordination procedure; the source writes the updated price as P(Q) and the fixed-point price as P*.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source; source treats it as a scalar regulated price","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e06"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","symbol":"Q","meaning":"Total utility electricity sales, equal to customers' consumption Q(P) in the price-response step of the fixed-point computation.","kind":"operational","domain":"not specified in source; source treats it as a scalar total sales/consumption quantity","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e05","v141-b-g05-e06"]}],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Repeat regulated-price updates and consumers’ responses, with the models solved again for power dispatch, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}.","expression":"P^{\\star} = P(Q(P^{\\star}))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-X001","v141-c-R003-X002"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e06","v141-b-g05-e05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Repeat the separate utility–microgrid decision iteration, in which the microgrid updates its decisions in response to the resulting regulated utility price, until convergence is achieved. Scope: Numerical equilibrium solution for Coalition {Private investor, D}, with separate utility and microgrid optimization models.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g05-e07"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Regulators may introduce feed-in premiums on top of market prices to promote microgrid development and efficient operation, including higher production at peak hours. Scope: Proposed regulatory incentives for microgrid development and efficient operation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e14"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p11-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":11,"printed_page":"534","section":"3.3 Scenario III: tripled MG’s capital costs","anchor_text":"value. sin This scenario is in the lower left corner of Fig. 2, with a negative ach private value an","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-11.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Regulators may introduce incentives (such as feed-in premiums) on top of market prices to promote the MG and its efficient operation (e.g. higher production at peak hours), as recommended by Scheepers et al. [69]. The costs of investing in an increasingly decentralized system may be socialized across all customers in the utility’s rate base. Regulators may also restructure rates to have MGs pay lower connection charges12 and/or demand charge rates, and impose higher remuneration rates for MG services to the grid.\nFinally, utilities may install a meter to measure electricity flows to and from the MG, and offer compensation for net excess generation from the microgrid through net metering, or 'running the meter backward'. The MG would thus effectively be paid the retail price, which includes not only the cost of energy, but also the cost of transmission and distribution. The use of net metering as pricing incentive to promote DG has been criticized, based on the argument that DG providers (and customers) should not receive compensation for distribution services that continue to be provided by the utility and should pay their share of the utility’s fixed costs for maintaining the distribution system [70,71]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In Coalitions {VIU}, {D}, {VIU, D}, {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D}, given an initial value for P, customers determine their consumption (equal to the utility’s sales), Q(P), and the model is solved to obtain the optimal power dispatch in the network. Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved. Note that in Coalitions {VIU, Private investor} and {VIU, Private investor, D} the regulated price is set to recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not subject to cost-of-service regulation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"} { , , } g p [17 recover the utility’s costs only, since the private investor is not sub- ject to c","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Solving Coalition {Private investor, D} introduces an additional complexity: the utility and the microgrid take decisions separately, but these decisions influence each other. Their optimization models are solved iteratively. In the first step, given an initial value for the utility’s regulated price, the microgrid optimizes electricity generation, imports from the main grid and reactive power production; the MG treats the utility’s regulated price as fixed. In the second step, the utility optimizes power dispatch in the network, given the microgrid’s quantity decisions; the VIU treats MG exports and generation as fixed. The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-43db3c92a3-p17-c1r2__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":17,"printed_page":"540","section":"Appendix C. Solution approach","anchor_text":"y g y q y sales (Eq. (3)). Thus, ﬁnding the equilibrium price and quantities is a ﬁxed point problem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-17.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Our variable of interest is the regulated electricity price, $P(Q)$. Given a continuous function mapping of $P$ into itself, $P(Q(P))$, the objective is to find a price $P^*$ such that $P^* = P(Q(P^*))$ and we achieve convergence to a fixed point. Convergence is not guaranteed, even if this exists; however, if the procedure converges, it will be to a fixed point. This iterative approach to finding a fixed point equilibrium has worked well in our study."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":"No parameter object is declared for this rule: the supplied coordination evidence states the fixed-point condition using endogenous price P and sales Q, and does not state a numerical convergence tolerance or other fixed parameter specific to this coordination procedure."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e06","quote":"Then, a new regulated electricity price, P(Q), is obtained, based on the new level of sales and costs. Given the price, consumers modify their decisions and the models are solved again to find the optimal power dispatch. The last two steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e07","quote":"The microgrid successively updates its decisions based on the resulting regulated price for the utility. These steps are repeated until convergence is achieved."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e14","quote":"Regulators may introduce incentives (such as feed-in premiums) on top of market prices to promote the MG and its efficient operation (e.g. higher production at peak hours), as recommended by Scheepers et al. [69]."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"f1a906415ff3c7a290c53a91315ab6c8ef73857440a86407d7b7bfb17ea6554c","applicability":"formalized","reason":"C001 is formalized as the source-supported fixed-point price-signal condition P* = P(Q(P*)), where Q(P) is customer consumption equal to utility sales and P(Q) is the updated regulated price recomputed from sales and costs. C002, the separate utility-microgrid decomposition, remains procedural because the supplied coordination text gives the iteration order but no explicit equation. C003 remains a source-stated regulatory policy recommendation, not a mathematical rule. P and Q are declared as endogenous operational variables because the source uses them in the price-signal iteration; their formal domains and units are not explicitly stated in the supplied text. All B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"P is the regulated price whose update and response constitute the coordination signal in the source's fixed-point procedure; it binds directly to the only approved node for this rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-X002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Q is the utility-sales/customer-consumption response driven by the regulated price signal; it binds to the same coordination.price_signal function."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The fixed-point condition formalizes the repeated regulated-price and consumption response as a constraint on the coordination.price_signal function; no graph edges were supplied."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The separate utility-microgrid iteration is a procedural branch of the same price-signal coordination function; no explicit equation or graph edge was supplied, so no expression is asserted."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C.1 attached the regulatory-incentive policy statement to coordination.price_signal; the source presents it as a possible policy recommendation, not a formal price-setting equation."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
